        "knowledge_point": "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097009/assets/1c42152de7cf176fa122ddf48aad7d93fe982ec1bc2717e3048d9ff5ea45f302.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{1}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(6).$",
        "worked_solution": "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=6,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097009/assets/145d9c16e27c67876d1ae2a00dce15b3cb7437f6a9121971fe4434e6c1fe943c.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [1,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [2,6],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [1,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [2,6],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(6)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}+4=\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
