        "knowledge_point_id": "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097078/assets/017fd41508a91d5674af92b280fd73690357645428279e09e8633fe70ce3278d.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        "worked_solution": "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [1,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =12+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
